=== REASONING ===

Why this design preserves ADR-015 + Phase-50 methodology:

  1. Default-off (§I1) preserved end-to-end.
     - Limiter and CostCap structs are passive: no goroutines started in
       NewLimiter / NewCostCap. The only background goroutine is in
       OllamaPoller, which is created only by the future router-wiring
       slice and is itself a no-op when ai_mode='off' (the poller checks
       mode at execution per §I12).
     - In off mode, the dispatcher is unreachable (guard.Wrap returns 404
       upstream of dispatch.Run). Even if a misconfigured route reached
       dispatch with a limit decorator wrapped around the provider, an
       off-mode call carries no Subject/FeatureID and the Limiter
       fail-loud paths (`missing_feature_id`) return without consuming
       any state.

  2. Baseline intact (§I3 / R8).
     - Limit-rejection is wired as a NORMAL terminal SSE event, not a
       hard error. Dispatcher catches *limit.LimitError via errors.As,
       calls the LimitErrorEmitter (the *stream.Writer satisfies the
       optional interface), and returns nil from Run. The page receives
       a structured `error` SSE frame with `baseline_available:true` and
       can pivot to its non-AI render path immediately. The AiLimitBanner
       surfaces this with a Retry button and an explicit "Use baseline"
       affordance the page wires to its non-AI fallback.

  3. Per-feature opt-in (§I7) — fail-loud on missing/unknown feature.
     - Limiter rejects unknown OR missing FeatureID with the typed
       reasons `unknown_feature_id` / `missing_feature_id`. A page that
       forgot to wire its feature ID into provider.WithFeatureID(ctx,
       "id") will see an immediate banner — never silent over-quota use.
     - Tier resolution goes through limit.TierResolver (interface) so
       internal/ai/limit does not import internal/ai/features. The
       production wiring slice supplies a shim around features.Registry.

  4. Zero outbound egress (§I4) preserved.
     - Decorators perform NO network calls. CostCap reads today's spend
       via the existing CostRepo (DB) interface; RateLimit is purely
       in-memory. The OllamaPoller probes ONLY the user-configured local
       URL (default http://localhost:11434/api/tags) — same surface F1
       already vetted.

  5. Decorator chain order (Pattern P5).
     - Source order in the future Chain() call:
         Chain(base, WithRedaction, WithRateLimit, WithCostCap,
               WithAudit, WithTrace)
     - Chain reduces left-to-right; first arg is INNERMOST wrap. So at
       runtime the OUTERMOST decorator is Trace and the INNERMOST is
       Redaction (closest to the wire). RateLimit sits between
       Redaction (innermost) and CostCap (next out) — i.e. CostCap runs
       BEFORE RateLimit. This matches the prompt's D10.3 rationale:
       CostCap is the cheaper LRU-cached check, and a cost-cap reject
       MUST NOT consume rate-limit budget.

  6. Strict cost-cap concurrency (R9 mitigation, with adaptation).
     - The Check call holds a per-subject mutex around the read-modify-
       write of `today + pending + estimate`. The estimate is reserved
       in `pending`; on Release the actual cost (or -1 = "use estimate")
       is deducted from `pending` and optimistically added to `today` so
       a burst of N concurrent calls correctly observes the cap before
       the 30s repo cache TTL refreshes. This avoids both the false-
       reject (estimate-doubled) and false-accept (race past cap) cases.

  7. Fail-CLOSED semantics on infra failure.
     - Settings lookup error → reason `settings_unavailable` (don't
       guess the cap; refuse).
     - CostRepo error → reason `cost_cap_unavailable` (don't pretend
       today is $0; refuse).
     - This is the more conservative direction; an open-fail would let
       a busted cost log effectively disable the cap.

  8. Stream goroutine pattern (lessons re-learned from F4/F8).
     - The stream-forwarding goroutine in BOTH new decorators uses an
       explicit two-arm select on `<-ctx.Done()` and `<-innerCh` (with
       comma-ok), NOT a `range innerCh` loop. The latter blocks on
       receive when ctx cancels and inner never closes — a real
       deadlock that surfaced in initial tests and was fixed.
     - The Limiter slot is released on the same goroutine that holds
       it, so a context-cancel cleanup never double-releases.

  9. WriteDone idempotency (defensive fix in the same slice).
     - WriteDoneFull previously returned ErrWriterClosed on a closed
       writer. The dispatcher's `defer w.WriteDone()` would then
       OVERWRITE the real return error with ErrWriterClosed after a
       limit-error early shutdown — an invisible bug that would have
       made the dispatcher swallow the LimitError code path silently.
     - Made WriteDoneFull return nil on already-closed; existing
       writer_test.go does not probe this case so the change is
       backward-safe.

  10. Decorators ship as building blocks (router wiring deferred).
     - The prompt allows changes to `internal/ai/dispatch/dispatch.go`
       and `internal/ai/stream/writer.go` but NOT to
       `internal/api/router.go` or `internal/ai/provider/registry.go`.
       This is intentional per Honesty Covenant rule 9 (no silent drift
       outside allowed files). F9 lands the decorators + tests as
       building blocks; the chain is wired into the registry by the
       first consuming feature slice (e.g. U1).
     - This mirrors the F1 precedent where some decorators (Audit,
       Trace) shipped before being added to the registry chain.
     - The dispatcher integration (errors.As in the Chat loop) IS
       active — once decorators are wired into the registry, limit
       errors will propagate without further dispatcher changes.

  11. Health poller decoupling.
     - internal/ai/health does NOT import internal/ai/limit. It uses
       narrow interfaces (Suspender, Doer, Clock) so *limit.Limiter
       satisfies Suspender duck-typed. This keeps the dependency graph
       acyclic and lets either package evolve independently.


